\title{Data Type Categories}
\description{
For promotion, every data type belongs to one of three categories, ordered
boolean < integer < float:
\itemize{
\item \strong{boolean} -- \code{bool}
\item \strong{integer} -- \code{i8}, \code{i16}, \code{i32}, \code{i64} and their unsigned counterparts
\code{ui8}, \code{ui16}, \code{ui32}, \code{ui64}
\item \strong{float} -- \code{f32} and \code{f64}.
}

These are the categories promotion works in, where signed and unsigned
integers count as one. \code{\link[xlamisc:dtype_category]{xlamisc::dtype_category()}} reports a finer split
that names \code{int} and \code{uint} separately.
}
\section{Data Type Vocabulary}{

Where a page says which data types an argument takes, it names a group of
them with a single word:
\itemize{
\item \emph{any data type} -- all of them: \code{bool}, the signed and unsigned integers,
and the floats.
\item \emph{numeric} -- signed integer, unsigned integer and float.
\item \emph{integer} -- signed and unsigned integer.
\item \emph{integerish} -- boolean and integer, signed or unsigned.
\item \emph{signed numeric} -- signed integer and float.
}
}

\section{Data Types for R Values}{

An R value has no data type of its own. Within its own category, it takes the
data type of the array it meets, and is built at it directly rather than
converted to it, which is what keeps \code{nv_scalar(1, "f64") / sqrt(2)} exact.
Where it meets nothing, it settles on the default of its category, which
\code{\link[=default_dtypes]{default_dtypes()}} reports and the \code{anvl.default_dtypes} option
configures. \code{\link[=peek_dtype]{peek_dtype()}} reports the data type a given R value would
take.

The same defaults are used wherever a function needs a data type that its
inputs do not give it. \code{nv_exp()} computes an integer input at the default
float, and \code{nv_add()} of an integer array and a double R value promotes to
the default float.

The primitives require operands that have a data type to agree on it; the
\verb{nv_*} functions promote them to a common one.
}

